  <ferc:ReportDate id="f-1" contextRef="c-1">2026-04-30</ferc:ReportDate>
  <ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="f-2" contextRef="c-1">&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:700;line-height:232%"&gt;1. ORGANIZATION&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:232%"&gt;In December 2013, an agreement was signed, effective January 1, 2014 whereby AES US Services, LLC (the "Service Company") a subsidiary of the The AES Corporation ("AES") began providing a range of services to specific affiliates of AES, otherwise known as the "Affiliates". These services include accounting, legal, human resources, information technology and other corporate services. In accordance with the AES U.S. Services, LLC Cost Alignment and Allocation Manual ("CAAM"), the Service Company allocates prudently-incurred costs to the Affiliates using cost drivers designed to achieve the following goals: prevent cross-subsidization of one entity by another, maximize synergies and economies of scale in the Service Company operations, and minimize the time and expense needed to record and audit the transactions. All costs incurred by the Service Company that are billed to the Affiliates are recovered at cost, and there is no "mark-up" or "profit".&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:232%"&gt;2. &#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:700;line-height:232%"&gt;SUMMARY OF SIGNIFICANT ACCOUNTING POLICIES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:700;line-height:232%;text-decoration:underline"&gt;Basis of Presentation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:232%"&gt; - The accompanying Balance Sheet and Income Statement of the Service Company found on pages 101-102 and 301-302 are prepared in accordance with the accounting requirements of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission ("FERC") as set forth in its applicable Uniform System of Accounts and published accounting releases, which is a comprehensive basis of accounting other than generally accepted accounting principles in the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:232%"&gt;United States ("GAAP"). The principle differences from GAAP in this FERC Form No. 60 are that it excludes a statement of cash flows and certain footnote disclosures that ordinarily would be required by GAAP.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:700;line-height:232%;text-decoration:underline"&gt;Use of Estimates&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:232%"&gt; - The preparation of financial statements in conformity with the accounting requirements of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission ("FERC") as set forth in its applicable Uniform System of Accounts requires that management make certain estimates and assumptions that affect the reported amounts of assets and liabilities and disclosure of contingent assets and liabilities at the date of the financial statements. The reported amounts of revenues and expenses during the reporting period may also be affected by the estimates and assumptions management is required to make. Actual results may differ from those estimates.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:232%"&gt;3.&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:700;line-height:232%"&gt;RELATED PARTY TRANSACTIONS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:232%"&gt;The Service Company renders services to the Affiliates which include accounting, legal, human resources, information technology and other corporate services. The cost for such services is allocated to the Affiliates based on cost drivers designed to result in fair and equitable allocations. This is accomplished, whenever possible and practical, through direct charges from the Service Company to appropriate specific Affiliates. When direct charging is not possible and practical, costs are allocated through allocations, based on a consistent method of determining cost causation from period to period, so that reasonable cost attribution occurs. Costs incurred to perform services that benefit more than one company are allocated to the benefiting companies using one of the FERC accepted allocation factors. The allocation factors used to bill for services performed by the Service Company are based upon formulas that &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:232%"&gt;consider factors such as numbers of customers, number of employees, number of invoices and other factors. The data upon which these formulas are based are updated annually. The billings for services to Affiliates are made at cost and include no compensation for a return on investment.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:232%"&gt;Under a fully allocated cost methodology, all expenditures such as labor, materials, and other related non-labor costs are charged directly to a single Affiliate or charged and then allocated among several Affiliates depending on the function performed. Based on the breakdown of direct labor hours, overhead charges including, but not limited to, health insurance, pension, and payroll taxes are charged to each Affiliate to arrive at the fully allocated cost. The resultant cost allocations for Affiliates and business activities to regulated and unregulated lines of business are based on an appropriate metric of cost causation for that business activity. This overall approach to cost allocation will be followed to the extent that it does not result in incurring a cost that is uneconomical in relation to the benefits achieved. Each Service Company area represents an organizational unit (division, department, cost center, etc.) that is responsible for its costs and for controlling and budgeting administratively assigned resources.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:232%"&gt;The Affiliates also charge certain costs to the Service Company. These are primarily related to labor and benefits of certain employees that remained as employees of a specific Affiliate yet perform work for multiple Affiliates. Another type of cost charged to the Service Company by Affiliates is occupancy costs (rents). The charges to the Service Company for this support are recorded at the higher of cost or market and are allocated using the same methodology as other Service Company costs.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:232%"&gt;In March 2016, the Indiana Utility Regulatory Commission (&#8220;IURC&#8221;), Cause No. 44576, approved Indianapolis Power &amp;amp; Light Company (&#8220;IPL&#8221; dba &#8220;AES Indiana&#8221;) basic rates case. The IURC found IPL&#8217;s Service Company cost allocations to be reasonable, however, IPL was directed to request that the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (&#8220;FERC&#8221;) review the Service Company allocations. In September 2017, the FERC completed its review, authorizing the Service Company&#8217;s allocation of costs of non-power goods and services to IPL. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:232%"&gt;Accounts Receivable primarily includes receivables from Affiliates for professional services rendered. The Service Company bills Affiliates for services rendered on a monthly basis based on a work order system. The transactions that give rise to these receivables and payables are settled monthly. The Affiliates generally remit these payments within 30 days.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:700;line-height:232%"&gt;4.&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;INCOME TAXES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:232%"&gt;The Service Company, as a subsidiary of AES, is included in the consolidated federal income tax return of AES. Under the LLC form of business, profits and losses at the Service Company flow through to the member and are not subject to tax at the LLC level. Accordingly, no provision for income taxes is made in the Service Company's financial statements.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:700;line-height:232%"&gt;5.&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;EMPLOYEE BENEFITS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:232%"&gt;Substantially all employees of the Service Company participate in a defined contribution plan, commonly referred to as a 401K. Many of these employees also receive retirement benefits under a DPL Inc., an indirect subsidiary of The AES Corporation, sponsored defined benefit plan. Most of the Service Company employees also receive health and life insurance benefits and may participate in other postretirement benefit plans sponsored by AES which provide &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:232%"&gt;health and life insurance benefits for retired employees. As described above in footnote 3, all costs allocated to the Service Company relating to such benefits are charged to the Affiliates in accordance with the CAAM.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock>
  <ferc:ReportDate id="f-3" contextRef="c-1">2026-04-30</ferc:ReportDate>
